A||N||7||cscougar||Coolant Temperature Guage Question||||1||
Z||000000||cscougar||07-22-2000||11:18 AM||||I transferred a working guage pod from my daughter's wrecked 85 2m6 into another vehicle.  The temperature guage is not working.  After disconnecting the sensor plug, I ground the green wire and it pegged the guage to the right.  However even with replacing the sensor with a new one, the guage does not work and stays all the way to the left.  Anyone have any suggestions on what else to do?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||07-22-2000||11:30 AM||||My gauge has a similar problem.  I have also replaced the guage and sender and it never gets above 100 degrees.  Since the senders resistance gets lower with temperature causing more current through the circuit, and thus the needle rises,  I figure that the wiring or connectors have a high impedance (corrosion or break) somewhere.<P>If it is the connectors, they can be scraped or cleaned enough to get it where it should<BR>make good contact.  If it is the wires, that is going to be a pain to find, fix and replace. ||||reg||11
Z||000002||terryk||07-22-2000||11:51 AM||||The sender has two outputs. One is the variable output for the gauge and the other is the temp lamp output. <P>Are you connecting them correctly? If you get them backwards, the lamp will glow and the gauge with only switch between zero scale and full scale. <P>On the sender:<P>Pin A Drk Grn/Wht is the gauge output.<BR>Pin B Drk Grn is the lamp output.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||07-22-2000||12:05 PM||||Be sure that you don't use teflon tape on the sensor -the shell has to be grounded.||||reg||1
Z||000004||cscougar||07-22-2000||02:55 PM||||The guage pegs to the right when I ground the green/white wire in the sensor connector.  But when I reconnect to the sensor then it doesn't read at all.  I think by pegging the guage with the grounding then its an indication that the guage and wiring work ok, just the sender is not working right.  I guess it could be a bad new sensor but I doubt that.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||general||08-24-2000||02:35 PM||||   I have been having the same type of problem with my tempguage .And just like you ,I have tried grounding the wires which end up making the tempguage needle peg.Since this is the case and since I have tried another guage with the same results,It make's you wonder if it would be better to go with an aftermarket tempguage.If this were done would it cause any problems with the readings picked up on the E.C.M. thereby causing it not to make proper adjustments in the fuel mixture etc.?    Any suggestions anyone???||||reg||9
Z||000006||theogre||08-24-2000||10:42 PM||||read what phil said again. you also can have trouble if RTV is on the threads.<P>The ECM has it's own sensor. It doesn't care about the gauge or light.||||reg||1
Z||000007||general||09-06-2000||01:46 PM||||   I went ahead and replaced the temp.guage with a mechanical guage which I mounted under the dash on the right side next to the center console.If anyone wants to try this you may want to make a spacer about 1/2"thick to make the guage easier to see from the drivers seat.Also be sure to get a guage with at least 96" of line going back to the sending unit.(120" would be easier and give you more choice as to where to run the line.)The light on the guage will tie into the extra wire coming off of the light switch on the left side of the dash.(Wire is white I believe.)Ground can be on the steering column bolt.The question that I still have is this:My fan on the radiator used to came on when I switched the car on,yet now it does'nt come on at all!I will begin testing out the fan and the wiring.But I want to ask just where is the sender located that turns on the fan on the radiator?    general||||reg||11
